  "narrative": "All newly delivered comparisons have valid Welch cells and adequately large group sizes. Estimates and intervals are interpreted as already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, and I use the 0.15 composite dependency-score-unit threshold in that direction, giving priority to the signed lower bound.\n\nH141 and H142 are the most important new findings. Both are very strong attenuation interactions for the feature_010 comparison in one-sided nonnegative assay contexts: H141 signed estimate +1.726 with signed lower bound +1.540, and H142 signed estimate +1.686 with signed lower bound +1.506. These far exceed the meaningful-effect threshold and support local heterogeneity of the global feature_010 higher-outcome association in contexts where the correlated standardized assays feature_038 and feature_018 are nonnegative. Because feature_038 and feature_018 are correlated constructed assays and multiple interaction forms were tested, these should be treated as assay-context heterogeneity rather than clean independent assay-specific modifications. H142 is accepted on discovery and closed as a redundant one-sided form. H141 is accepted but kept active because I request independent validation of this boundary; if validation is not decisive, the already validated broader attenuation forms H110/H120/H122 should remain the main attenuation claims.\n\nH143, H145, and H146 were registered with the global feature_010 direction of higher outcome_001, but the signed evidence is strongly opposite: in the joint feature_038>=0 and feature_018>=0 context, and in triple-positive or feature_036-negative subcontexts within that joint positive setting, feature_010=1 is associated with lower outcome_001 by about 0.83-0.85 units. The registered positive claims are therefore rejected. This is not evidence against the global feature_010 positive association; rather, it identifies a local sign reversal inside a highly selected nonnegative assay context. The new proposal reverses H143 to test this local negative association directly. The feature_036 split does not explain the reversal because H145 and H146 are similar.\n\nH144 is near null for a feature_036-specific interaction inside the joint positive assay context, consistent with H145/H146. It is rejected and closed.\n\nH147 and H148 are strong specificity controls. In the triple nonnegative assay context, feature_037=1 remains associated with lower outcome_001 (signed lower bound +0.842) and feature_002=1 remains associated with higher outcome_001 (signed lower bound +0.872). This supports local specificity: the central feature_037 and feature_002 associations persist even where the feature_010 association is attenuated or locally reversed. These are dataset-internal associations and not proof of independent mechanistic effects.\n\nAmong the broad screens, H27 (feature_063) has a point estimate above threshold (+0.227) but a signed lower bound slightly below 0.15 (+0.131), so it is not established as meaningful; it remains unresolved and deferred. H28, H29, and H69 are near null or inconsistent with the claimed direction and are rejected and closed.\n\nOverall, the central axes remain: feature_037=1 with lower outcome_001, feature_010=1 with higher outcome_001, and feature_002=1 with higher outcome_001. The key refinement is that the feature_010 association is strongly context-dependent in positive or jointly nonnegative feature_038/feature_018 assay contexts, with evidence for attenuation and possible local reversal. All conclusions remain associations within the supplied dataset and do not establish causality, mechanism, gene-level biology, clinical relevance, or external generalizability."
,
  "research_notes": "Post iteration 11 appraise. Central validated main axes unchanged: feature_037=1 lower outcome_001, feature_010=1 higher outcome_001, feature_002=1 higher outcome_001. New H141/H142 one-sided attenuation interactions are extremely large and locally support feature_010 attenuation in nonnegative feature_038/feature_018 contexts. H141 is accepted active and sent for independent validation to decide whether the one-sided boundary is preferable to broader validated forms H110/H120/H122. H142 accepted closed as redundant. H143/H145/H146 reject the positive-direction feature_010 claims inside joint positive assay contexts but reveal a strong local negative feature_010 association; propose reversal H149 as parent of H143. H144 rejects feature_036-specific attenuation within that context; feature_036 remains weak/unstable. H147/H148 accept persistence of feature_037 lower-outcome and feature_002 higher-outcome in triple-positive context, supporting feature_010-specific heterogeneity. H27 is borderline: point above 0.15 but lower bound 0.131; unresolved/deferred after validation capacity is spent on H141. H28/H29/H69 null. Continue to treat feature_038/feature_018 as correlated assays; avoid attributing attenuation to one assay after adjustment. Next analyze the reversed H149 feature_010 lower-outcome claim inside joint nonnegative feature_038/feature_018. If H141 validation is decisive, report a one-sided attenuation boundary; if not, retain H110/H120/H122 as main validated attenuation claims. Avoid accepting H27 or feature_003/feature_058 tail patterns without stronger evidence. All claims remain dataset-internal associations, not causal or generalizable."
